.Dd $Mdocdate: August 07 2026 $
.Dt EXPERIMENTS 0
.Os ALL
.Sh NAME
.Nm experiments
.Nd Eurorack System module
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
is using a
.Ar DDS
running on a
.Ar stm32g071xx
using the internal DAC/ADC so no need for coplex protocols, everything is
handled on the chip. Hence the resolution and tracking accuracy of the
device descussed at
.Ar CAPABILITIES.
The full DAC of the SoC is used to allow for any
kind of mixing possibilities (i.e. L/R, independent outputs).

By design the flash has approximatly 90kBytes for tables of one
.Ar period
of a signal/waveform. The basic 4 are already baked in for debugging also.
.Pp
Building binary files and also flashing the device is handled by
.Ar run_target()
from Meson. We chose Meson to generate the
.Ar ninja.build
file and then all is handled by ninja. If you are reading this you already 
have the source tree for the project with the submodules populated so next
thing to fetch the dependencies.

.Sh BUILD INSTRUCTIONS
The following dependencies should be installed by the user and added to the
environment variable for the shell to be able to find them. Those are:

.Ar Meson | Ninja | st-utils | arm-none-eabi-gcc
compiler collection

Firstly generate the build directory with all neccessary things. Run this on
the root folder of the project `$home/your/path/to/experiments`

.Bl -tag -width stopped -offset indent -compact
.It Cm meson setup build --cross-file crossing.txt
Create all neccessary build related files inside `build/` located at the top level. 

If the command above failed Meson will error out with a message instead of an exit
code (Nice!). The most propable cause will be something missing or
.Ar git submodules
not properly populated.
.El

.Pp
To build binaries for flashing on the mcu run one of the comands bellow.

.Bl -tag -width stopped -offset indent -compact
.It Cm ninja Ar testershit
optimized for size (i.e. -Osize)

.It Cm ninja Ar release
with aggressive
.Ar -O3 optimization
.El
.Pp
To send a binary to the flash use the following commands
.Ar respectively.
Using ST-Link(TM).

.Bl -tag -width stopped -offset indent -compact
.It Cm ninja Ar sendit
send size optimized binary to flash.

.It Cm ninja Ar fast
send aggressive optimized binary to flash.
.El
.Sh CAPABILITIES
Using most of the on chip mechanisms,
.Ar 6
of the ADC channels are utilized, gathering input data to modulate waveform
parameters. Those are sampled using a internal timer keeping the interval at
.Ar world.adc1_clock
= 48 [kHz]. This is never exactly 48k but rather computed using a prescaler
sourcing the main internal clock for the timer peripheral. More can be found for
the mechanism of clocking a ST(TM) in the sources of
.Ar LL
drivers for the timer. Practically you end up with a clock around 39.7-39.9
[kHz]. This will be measured on the final device.
.Ss waveforms
Wave forms are stored in single dimentional arrays of 128 samples with a maximum
amplitude value 0xfff (4095). We do not use absolute max/min due to bad
sinusoidal effects with too much clipping. The four usual suspects (sin, saw up,
saw down and square) are all generated by Dr. LUT (see
.Ar REFERENCES )

.Ss bandwidth
Approximatly the 54.5% of the audible bandwidth is used. Untill now the highest
frequency reliably produced is 12 [kHz] and the device maximum limit is there.
We may be able to increase it when there is a more intelligent decimation
algorithm for the waveform tables to reduce DAC overruns/underruns.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Examples of using the api can be found in
.Ar tune()
at src/overseer.c. Here we will descuss some patching and sound design examples. 

.Bl -tag -width stopped -offset indent -compact
.It Cm Free and tracking mode
Tracking will be used to apply a bandwidth limit and give more hands-on behavior
without jumping too much.

.It Cm FM modulation
Patching either the second output to the first input or the opposite will use the
one as modulator and the other as a carrier. This does not have any meaning when
the two outputs are synced (L/R mode).

.It Cm PD modulation
Phase modulation is handled by the device. In a DDS you achive this by changing
the slope of the accumulator. If this does not make sence
.Ar do not panic.
It didn't to me also at the beginning. At the root, a DDS is using the overflow
of an accumulated value as an index to walk a table.

Well
.Ar there
is your answer! Recording and plotting this number at the y'y axis and the time at
x'x you get a linear increase with a
.Ar slope .
Makes more sence when you read a proper book about it but the fundamental concept
boils down to the above.

.It Cm PD and FM combined
That is my favorite and as frequency increases you can almost mamble ...

It's Alive!

.El
.Sh REFERENCES
(1)   https://ppelikan.github.io/drlut/


